##* {{quote-song|en|date=2016-02-14|author=w:[[Skepta|Joseph Adenuga]]; w:[[D Double E|Darren Dixon]]; w:[[ASAP Nast|Tariq Devega]]|title=w:[[Ladies Hit Squad (song)|Ladies Hit Squad]]|album=w:[[Konnichiwa (Skepta album)|Konnichiwa]]|track=6|artist={{w|Skepta}}|feat=w:D Double E; w:ASAP Nast|publisher=w:Boy Better Know|passage='''Back''' so big, look like your jeans shrunk in the wash / And we don't really need Netflix, I'mma give you something to watch}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}} The part of a piece of clothing which covers the back.
##: {{ux|en|I still need to finish the '''back''' of your dress.}}
## {{senseid|en|furniture backrest}} The [[backrest]], the part of a piece of furniture which receives the human back.
##: {{ux|en|Can you fix the '''back''' of this chair?}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} That part of the body that bears clothing. (Now used only in the phrase {{l|en|clothes on one's back}}.)
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1604|author=w:William Shakespeare|title=s:Measure for Measure| passage=Do thou but think / What ’tis to cram a maw or clothe a '''back''' / From such a filthy vice}}
# That which is [[farthest]] away from the front.
#: {{ux|en|He sat in the '''back''' of the room.}}
## The side of any object which is opposite the [[front]] or useful side.
##: {{ux|en|Turn the book over and look at the '''back'''.}}
### {{senseid|en|bound edge of book}} The edge of a book which is [[bind|bound]].
###: {{ux|en|The titles are printed on the '''backs''' of the books.}}
### {{lb|en|printing}} The inside [[margin]] of a page.
###* {{quote-book|en|year=1841|author={{w|William Savage (printer)|William Savage}}| page=472| year_published=1965|publisher=Ayer Publishing| title=A Dictionary of the Art of Printing|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=sS4H3hax5hAC| isbn=0833731289| passage=Convenience and custom have familiarised us to the printed page being a little higher than the middle of the leaf, and to its having a little more margin at the fore edge than in the '''back'''.}}
### The side of a [[blade]] opposite the side used for cutting.
###: {{ux|en|Tap it with the '''back''' of your knife.}}
## The [[reverse]] side; the side that is not normally seen.
##: {{ux|en|I hung the clothes on the '''back''' of the door.}}
## Area behind, such as the [[backyard]] of a house or the rear [[storeroom]] of a retail [[store#Noun|store]].
##: {{ux|en|We’ll meet out in the '''back''' of the library.}}
##: {{ux|en|Are you sure you're totally out of hair gel? Could you please check in the '''back'''?}}
## The part of something that goes [[last]].
##: {{ux|en|The car was near the '''back''' of the train.}}
## {{lb|en|sports}} In some [[team sport]]s, a position behind most players on the team.
##: {{ux|en|The '''backs''' were lined up in an I formation.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=December 28, 2010|author=Kevin Darlin|work=BBC| title=West Brom 1-3 Blackburn|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_prem/9308862.stm| passage={{...}}Rovers were also aided by some poor defending from West Brom, whose lapses at the '''back''' undid their excellent work on the ball and condemned Roberto di Matteo's Baggies side to a third straight defeat.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} The upper part of a natural object which is considered to resemble an animal’s back.
#: {{ux|en|The small boat raced over the '''backs''' of the waves.}}
# A support or resource in reserve.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|act=IV|scene=vii|passage=This project / Should have a '''back''' or second, that might hold, / If this should blast in proof.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} The [[keel]] and [[keelson]] of a ship.
#: {{ux|en|The ship’s '''back''' broke in the pounding surf.}}
# {{lb|en|mining}} The roof of a horizontal underground passage.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1911|author=Robert Bruce Brinsmade| title=Mining Without Timber|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=34VCAAAAIAAJ| page=161| passage=The stope is kept full of broken ore, sufficient only being drawn to leave a working space between the floor of broken ore and the '''back''' of the stope.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|uncountable}} Effort, usually physical.
